/**
 * @page xpost_design Design
 *
 * xpost is a PostScript LanguageLevel 3 interpreter built as a shared
 * library with programs over it. The design holds to a few commitments
 * throughout: every allocation is indexed rather than addressed, so
 * storage can be rearranged, snapshotted and given back; the language's
 * own machinery is written in PostScript wherever PostScript can
 * express it, over a small sealed core, with C reserved for what the
 * language cannot say and for the paths where speed is the point;
 * memory for a page is bounded by policy rather than by the page's
 * size; and every structural rule the design states is enforced by a
 * test that fails when the rule is broken, so this description and the
 * tree cannot quietly diverge.
 *
 * Two companion pages carry the enumerations this one refers to rather
 * than repeats: @subpage xpost_roots, the garbage collector's root set
 * bank by bank, and @subpage xpost_dicts, which dictionary every name
 * lives in and the rule for adding one. doc/MANUAL is the user's side,
 * and doc/GATING.md describes the test architecture the last commitment
 * rests on.
 *
 * @section design_toc Contents
 *
 * @li @ref design_langsplit
 * @li @ref design_modules
 * @li @ref design_boot
 * @li @ref design_objects
 * @li @ref design_vm
 * @li @ref design_stacks
 * @li @ref design_names
 * @li @ref design_dicts
 * @li @ref design_save
 * @li @ref design_gc
 * @li @ref design_free
 * @li @ref design_return
 * @li @ref design_loop
 * @li @ref design_scanner
 * @li @ref design_errors
 * @li @ref design_jobs
 * @li @ref design_contexts
 * @li @ref design_devices
 * @li @ref design_imaging
 * @li @ref design_colour
 * @li @ref design_vector
 * @li @ref design_banding
 * @li @ref design_spill
 * @li @ref design_fonts
 * @li @ref design_resources
 * @li @ref design_files
 * @li @ref design_forms
 * @li @ref design_confinement
 * @li @ref design_dsc
 * @li @ref design_testing
 * @li @ref design_perf
 * @li @ref design_practice
 * @li @ref design_distinctives
 * @li @ref xpost_roots
 * @li @ref xpost_dicts
 *
 * @section design_langsplit What is C and what is PostScript
 *
 * The C core implements what the language cannot express about itself:
 * the memory files and their tables, objects, the segmented stacks,
 * the save machinery, the garbage collector, the scanner, the operator
 * table and its dispatch, files and filters, and the compiled halves
 * of the output devices. Everything above that is PostScript, loaded
 * at boot from the data directory: the error mechanism (err.ps),
 * resources, and the whole of the graphics language -- paths,
 * painting, colour spaces, clipping, images, patterns, shadings,
 * halftones, the device classes and the band loop -- in callout.ps
 * and the files it loads. The interpreter runs without any of the
 * graphics loaded (\--no-graphics), and the graphics arrive lazily the
 * first time something asks for them.
 *
 * The load chain is concrete and shallow: init.ps builds the
 * machinery and loads the error mechanism, the sort and resource
 * machinery and the job layer; callout.ps, when asked for, loads
 * the device layer, the graphics state, colour, paths, clipping,
 * painting, text, patterns and shadings; the device layer loads the
 * imaging core, the structural devices and the writers; and the
 * imaging core loads the raster format classes. Each file is loaded
 * through one module loader, so where a file may be found is decided
 * in one place. The interpreter locates its data directory by a
 * five-step search -- an environment override, the directory derived
 * from the shared library's own location, the source tree recorded
 * at configure time, the installed data directory, and finally the
 * working directory's neighbourhood -- which is what makes an
 * uninstalled build self-sufficient from any working directory.
 *
 * The boundary is guarded on both sides. systemdict is sealed in C
 * once boot completes, so a program cannot redefine what the core
 * depends on. The PostScript machinery keeps its own state in private
 * dictionaries off the dictionary stack, reached by explicit fetch
 * rather than by name lookup, and its dispatch tables hold operator
 * values rather than names -- so a program that redefines a standard
 * operator changes what programs see without changing what the
 * machinery does. The compiled helpers the graphics use (the scanline
 * fills, the row blitter, the sorters, the compressors) live under dot
 * names in an internal dictionary that is not on any lookup path;
 * nothing of the machinery's is defined where a program could shadow
 * it. So systemdict holds the whole published language -- every
 * operator the language defines is there, and none of the interpreter's
 * own machinery is (bar six names the C reaches by name) -- and userdict
 * and globaldict start empty, so a program begins with a clean namespace
 * and adds only its own. This is hygiene rather than a security
 * boundary: it makes the interpreter robust against ordinary
 * redefinition and keeps the namespace a job sees empty of
 * implementation detail.
 *
 * Within the graphics, the split between the two languages is drawn by
 * profiling rather than by principle. The raster device classes are
 * PostScript -- one shared method suite parameterised per format --
 * and stay PostScript where a method runs once per page; the methods
 * that run once per mark, per span or per pixel are compiled, and are
 * swapped into a class as an instance is made. A rectangle fill, the
 * scanline polygon fill, the pixel blenders, the row writers, the
 * image-row blitter and the base64 and flate encoders are C; the logic
 * that decides what to fill, where the page begins and ends, and what
 * a device promises, is PostScript. The swap is conditional and
 * honest: a compiled fill that writes the class's array of rows is
 * only installed on a device that has such an array, a device with a
 * buffer of its own keeps its own compiled methods, and the null
 * device keeps a no-op fill so that discarded pages are not rendered
 * first and thrown away after.
 *
 * One process holds one interpreter, which xpost_create() returns an
 * execution context of and xpost_destroy() ends. The @c fork operator
 * makes further contexts inside the same interpreter; a fork shares
 * both memory files and gets stacks of its own.
 *
 * @section design_modules The modules, and how they layer
 *
 * Two module sets, one per language. Every C source opens with a doxygen
 * block naming itself and saying what it holds -- the file list of this
 * reference is generated from those, and a guard
 * (tests/check-file-purpose.sh) fails a source that carries no such block,
 * so the map below cannot fall behind the tree without something going
 * red.
 *
 * The C side layers from the arena upward, and the dependency runs one
 * way: the core knows nothing of objects, objects know nothing of
 * operators, and the operator modules know nothing of each other.
 *
 * @verbatim
 * core        xpost_memory     the arena, and the table that indexes it
 *             xpost_stack      the segmented stacks
 *             xpost_save       save and restore
 *             xpost_free       the free lists
 *             xpost_garbage    the collector  (+ _diag, what it reports)
 *             xpost_handle     naming a block that is not in virtual memory
 *             xpost_vm_image   a context's virtual memory as one file
 *
 * objects     xpost_object     tags, and the simple constructors
 *             xpost_array      a run of objects in the arena
 *             xpost_string     a run of bytes in the arena
 *             xpost_dict       the hash table every name is looked up in
 *             xpost_name       the trees that give each name a number
 *             xpost_file       disk files, and the filters over one
 *             xpost_operator   the operator table, in row order
 *
 * run         xpost_interpreter  the main loop
 *             xpost_context      the stacks, the two banks, the roots
 *             xpost_error        raising, and the record a handler reads
 *             xpost_main         starting a run and taking it down
 *             xpost_log          where the interpreter's own messages go
 *             xpost_compat       what the hosts disagree about (+ _posix,
 *                                _win32)
 *
 * operators   xpost_oplib      asks each module below to install what it owns
 *             xpost_op_stack   xpost_op_boolean  xpost_op_control
 *             xpost_op_math    xpost_op_array    xpost_op_string
 *             xpost_op_dict    xpost_op_type     xpost_op_save
 *             xpost_op_file    xpost_op_token    xpost_op_misc
 *             xpost_op_path    xpost_op_matrix   xpost_op_font
 *             xpost_op_param   xpost_op_context  xpost_op_packedarray
 *
 * imaging     xpost_span       a row of pixels a paint covers
 *             xpost_matrix     the transformations a graphics state holds
 *             xpost_font       faces, glyphs, and the rendered-glyph cache
 *             xpost_record     the recorded page
 *             xpost_spill      the scratch file such a page may go to
 *             xpost_strbuf     a growing byte buffer, outside virtual memory
 *
 * devices     xpost_dev_generic  what every other device inherits from
 *             xpost_dev_driver   loads a device by name
 *             xpost_dev_record   keeps the marks instead of the pixels
 *             xpost_dev_raster   the embedder's rasters, per pixel layout
 *             xpost_dev_bgr      the BGR raster
 *             xpost_dev_png      PNG, with and without alpha
 *             xpost_dev_jpeg     JPEG
 *             xpost_dev_xcb      the X11 window, through XCB
 *             xpost_dev_win32    the Windows window
 *
 * dsc         xpost_dsc_parse    reads the structuring comments
 *             xpost_dsc_file     gets a file's bytes to that reader
 *
 * programs    xpost              the command-line interpreter
 *             xpost_dsc          prints what the comments say
 *             xpost_view         puts a rendered page in a window
 *                                (+ _xcb, _win32 back ends)
 *             xpost_client       drives the interpreter as a library
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * The PostScript side is the files in the data directory, loaded
 * through one module loader. init.ps builds the machinery and loads
 * err.ps, the sort and resource machinery (qsort.ps, resource.ps) and the
 * job layer; callout.ps, when first asked for, loads the graphics
 * language in a fixed order -- device, gstate, halftone, color, path,
 * clip, paint, font, cid, pattern, shade -- and the device layer loads the
 * imaging core (image.ps), the structural devices (nulldev.ps, bboxdev.ps,
 * recorddev.ps), the raster format classes (pgmimage, ppmimage, pbmimage,
 * tiffimage) and the vector writers (pdfwrite, svgwrite, dscwrite).
 * prepr.ps is the pre-parser the scanner's binary token work uses. The
 * order is load-bearing where a module bakes a reference to another's
 * helper, which is why it is stated once, in callout.ps, rather than
 * discovered per file.
 *
 * Neither set has a unit-test main() left in it: the six that once lived
 * behind @c TESTMODULE_* symbols are gone, and every test that runs is in
 * tests/.
 *
 * @section design_boot Booting, and fast initialisation from an image
 *
 * A cold boot interprets about a megabyte and a third of PostScript --
 * init.ps, the error machinery, and on demand the graphics chain --
 * into virtual memory, then seals systemdict and locks the machinery
 * down. Every launch of the same build does exactly the same work to
 * reach exactly the same state, which is what makes the fast path
 * possible: the state can be written once and read back.
 *
 * A virtual-memory image is both banks written whole -- the bytes of
 * each arena, the table that indexes each, the arena's own
 * bookkeeping, and the few members of the context that name what the
 * arenas hold (which stacks are which, the allocation mode, the
 * one-shot flags the lockdown spent). Virtual memory is
 * position-independent by construction -- every reference in it is an
 * entity number or a byte offset, never an address -- so nothing in an
 * image needs relocating: a run that has one pays for reading a file
 * instead of for running the boot files. The image is written at the
 * point where the language stands complete -- after the graphics load
 * and the lockdown, before any device exists -- and only from the
 * first context a process makes. XPOST_VM_IMAGE names the file to
 * read, XPOST_VM_IMAGE_WRITE the file to write, and
 * XPOST_NO_VM_IMAGE turns reading off without moving the file.
 *
 * A run told nothing looks in two places -- beside the boot files, then
 * in the user's cache directory (@c XDG_CACHE_HOME or @c $HOME/.cache,
 * @c LOCALAPPDATA on Windows) -- and, finding neither, writes the
 * second. The first is an installation's: built once by whoever
 * assembled it, out of the files sitting beside it, read by every run
 * on the machine and never written by one. The second is a cache in the
 * ordinary sense, and deleting it costs the time it would have saved.
 * The file's name carries the object width and the configuration,
 * because those decide what the bytes mean and which language was
 * built, and deliberately not the identity of the build, so two builds
 * of one width and configuration take turns at one file rather than
 * each leaving one behind for ever. A run that built the language
 * writes through a file named for its own process and moves it onto the
 * name, so another run finds a whole image or none. The suite sets
 * XPOST_NO_VM_IMAGE throughout: which of the two ways a run comes up
 * would otherwise depend on whether a file happened to be sitting in
 * the cache, and a suite whose subject is the language must not answer
 * differently for that reason. The image has tests of its own, which
 * say where their image is.
 *
 * What an image does not carry is as much of the design as what it
 * does. Host state is excluded by construction: where this process
 * mapped the arenas and which descriptors are open are this process's
 * own. The arena itself holds no host address at all -- the operator
 * table, which is where they were, is the memory file's own storage
 * and no part of it -- so the bytes go out as they stand and nothing
 * is put back at the read.
 *
 * What the image does carry of that table is its virtual-memory half:
 * per row, the name-stack index of the operator's name, the procedure
 * a wrapped operator runs, and the operand shapes of the operators the
 * boot files declare in PostScript. A run that reads an image never
 * runs those files, so without them it would come up with wrapped
 * operators that run nothing. The rows a build installs from C keep
 * the signatures that build installed, function pointers and all.
 *
 * The image carries the operator names in row order and the read holds
 * this build's table to them row for row; an operator object carries
 * nothing but its row number, so a table in another order would
 * dispatch to the wrong operator, and the check is what makes that
 * impossible rather than unlikely. A
 * block held outside virtual memory (a device's state, a stream, a
 * face) is named in VM by a handle only this process can resolve, so
 * an image is refused at the write if any entity carries one: nothing
 * in a written image stands for a thing only the writing process had.
 *
 * An image is a picture of one build's memory, not a portable
 * document, and it says so up front: its head carries stamps for the
 * object width, the sizes of every structure the arena holds at a
 * fixed shape, the byte order, the build version, and a hash of every
 * boot file the language was assembled from. The last four bytes are
 * a digest of all the rest. A stamp that disagrees, a digest that
 * does not check, a file that is short, absent or foreign -- every
 * way an image can be unusable ends the same way: the read declines
 * with the context untouched, and the run boots from the files. A
 * refusal costs the time the image would have saved and nothing else,
 * which is what lets the mechanism default to being trusted with
 * nothing: the digest catches damage, not forgery, and an image wants
 * exactly the trust the boot files beside it already need.
 *
 * @section design_objects Objects
 *
 * A PostScript object is a small tagged value: a tag word, and fields
 * whose meaning the tag selects. A composite object (array, string,
 * dictionary, file) carries four -- tag, size, ent, offset -- where
 * @c ent indexes a memory table rather than addressing memory, and
 * the size and offset make @c getinterval a field adjustment rather
 * than a copy. Simple objects carry their payload in the same space:
 * an integer is tag plus a value word, an operator holds the number
 * of its row in the operator table, a name holds its index in the
 * name string stack. The tag also carries the access attributes, the
 * literal/executable flag, the bank flag saying which memory file a
 * composite lives in, and a flag distinguishing packed procedures --
 * which is what lets @c bind tell a packed procedure it should
 * descend into from a read-only array it should leave alone.
 *
 * The tree builds at two widths, and both are first-class: the whole
 * test suite runs at each. The default build gives each object field
 * 16 bits; the large-object build (-Dlarge-object=true) gives it 32,
 * and widens the integer to 64 bits and the real to double. The
 * bounds that follow:
 *
 *   field width          16 bit          32 bit
 *   composite length     65,535          4,294,967,295
 *   entity numbers       524,287         2,147,483,647
 *   integer              32 bit          64 bit
 *   real                 float           double
 *
 * The narrow composite bound is the architectural limit PLRM
 * Appendix B.1 documents; the entity range is wider than the length
 * field because the entity number borrows the tag's spare bits.
 *
 * Packed procedures are the language's compact, read-only procedure
 * form (@c setpacking selects them; the scanner then packs the
 * procedures it builds). Here they are ordinary arrays wearing the
 * packed flag: the compactness is the arena's business, and the
 * flag's real work is telling @c bind that this read-only procedure
 * is one it should descend into and freeze, so deeply nested
 * generated procedures bind all the way down.
 *
 * @section design_vm Virtual memory: the arena and its table
 *
 * Virtual memory is two banks -- local and global -- each an
 * Xpost_Memory_File: one large region, called the arena, sub-allocated
 * through an Xpost_Memory_Table sitting at its base. Global memory
 * holds what survives save and restore -- the frozen library, fonts
 * and resources; local memory holds the job's
 * own allocations and is what the save machinery rewinds. A global
 * container may not hold a reference to a local object (local memory
 * is the perishable bank, and such a reference would dangle);
 * local may hold global freely. Allocation goes to the bank the
 * current VM mode selects.
 *
 * The region is obtained by whichever of three arrangements the host
 * offers, all chosen for the same property -- the base never moves as
 * the file grows:
 *
 *   a mapping extended in place      mmap with mremap
 *   a reservation committed piecewise  mmap without mremap, and Win64
 *   the host allocator               a host with neither
 *
 * The middle arrangement reserves the whole range an offset can
 * address without being charged for it, and commits more (in one-MiB
 * steps) as the file fills. Under the allocator the region grows by a
 * factor of one and a half plus the request, and the grow may copy;
 * under the first two it never does. Only the first two own the pages
 * beneath the arena, so only they can give storage back (see
 * @ref design_return). The configure summary reports which
 * arrangement a build has as its "memory backing", and each is
 * exercised deliberately: -Dmmap=false forces the allocator,
 * -Dmremap=false forces the reservation, and XPOST_GROW_MOVES forces
 * a moving grow, which is how the relocation discipline is stress
 * tested on a host whose mappings would otherwise never move.
 *
 * Every "pointer" within the arena is an integer byte-offset from the
 * base, and every allocation is 8-aligned with the arena's cursor
 * advanced by the aligned extent. The coding rule that follows -- do
 * not hold a C pointer across an allocation -- is what the whole
 * design buys its freedom with: because nothing holds addresses,
 * storage can move (compaction), be written out and read back (the
 * image), and be handed back to the operating system (page return).
 *
 * @image html mtab.png
 * @image latex mtab.eps
 *
 * The figure is the memory file. @c base is the address the process
 * mapped the region at -- the one datum that is host state rather
 * than arena state. At offset zero of the region sits the table.
 * Its header carries @c nextent, the number the next fresh row will
 * get (a counter that only rises); @c max, the number of row slots
 * allocated (doubling from a thousand as rows are spent); and the
 * sixteen free-list heads (see @ref design_free). Each row describes
 * one entity -- one allocated thing:
 *
 *   @c adr    where the entity's bytes begin, as an offset from base
 *   @c used   how many of those bytes are in use -- an entity may
 *             occupy a larger block than it needs, when it was served
 *             from the free list
 *   @c sz     the size of the whole allocated block
 *   @c mark   the collector's mark bits and the save machinery's
 *             level bookkeeping; for file entities, the birth stamp
 *             (see @ref design_save)
 *   @c tag    what kind of thing the block holds, where the collector
 *             or the handle machinery needs to know -- it is how the
 *             collector closes a file it reclaims, and how a block
 *             holding a handle on host state is recognised
 *
 * A sixth field, @c nextfree, links rows into the free lists; it
 * lives in the row precisely so that freed storage itself holds
 * nothing the allocator reads. The cursor -- the @c used field of the
 * file itself, distinct from any row's -- is where live storage ends
 * and the next fresh allocation begins. @c vmstatus reports it, which
 * is why that figure is a high-water mark in ordinary running and
 * falls only when compaction closes the arena up.
 *
 * The first several rows of each bank are special entities, built
 * once at boot and serving as infrastructure rather than allocations:
 * the free-list heads (FREE), the save stack (VS), the list of
 * contexts sharing the file (CTXLIST), the name string stack and name
 * tree (NAMES, NAMET), and a distinguished string for the
 * name-not-found answer (BOGUSNAME). Both banks hold the same six.
 * The collector's domain begins just past them -- the memory file records that boundary as
 * its @c start -- so the special entities are roots by construction,
 * not things to be marked and swept.
 *
 * An allocation, end to end: the free lists are consulted first (a
 * bounded search; see @ref design_free); failing that, a row number
 * is taken -- preferably from the list of rows whose storage
 * compaction took, so spent numbers are reissued rather than leaked
 * -- and fresh bytes are taken at the cursor, growing the region if
 * the cursor would pass its end. The last stretch of entity numbers
 * is held in reserve for the machinery that reports errors, so a
 * program that exhausts the table is refused with a @c limitcheck it
 * can catch (the width of the number is an implementation limit;
 * memory was not actually spent) rather than failing inside the
 * failure report.
 *
 * @section design_stacks Stacks
 *
 * @image html stack.png
 * @image latex stack.eps
 *
 * Stacks are chains of fixed-size segments -- a thousand objects each
 * -- living in the memory files, each segment an entity with a row of
 * its own, naming its neighbours by entity number. In the figure,
 * @c nextseg is that link, and @c top counts how many slots of the
 * segment are in use; the topmost value of the whole stack lives in
 * the last segment at slot top-1. The root segment also names the
 * segment currently holding the top, so reaching the top of a deep
 * stack costs no walk, and a segment emptied by popping is kept
 * chained for the next push to move back into rather than being
 * given up.
 *
 * The operand, execution, dictionary and hold stacks belong to the
 * context, not to a memory file, so contexts sharing memory keep
 * independent execution state. The hold stack is a working area the
 * operator dispatch moves arguments into: bounded to one segment, it
 * can be read as a flat array, which is what lets a C operator
 * receive its operands as ordinary arguments. The save stack, by
 * contrast, belongs to the bank itself -- what it records is the
 * bank's history, not any context's.
 *
 * Pushes and pops resolve the segment chain as they go, but the
 * common runs are batched: a run of pushes resolves the root and top
 * segment once and copies the whole run through held pointers, and
 * @c roll, @c copy and @c counttomark snapshot the top run in one
 * pass, so each is linear in the depth it touches.
 *
 * @section design_names Names
 *
 * Names are interned: the characters are stored once per bank, and a
 * name object carries only an index. Each bank holds a ternary search
 * tree mapping strings to indices and a name string stack holding the
 * reverse mapping, both as special entities. All tree nodes live in
 * one entity and name each other by node number, exactly as objects
 * name storage by entity number, so the tree survives the arena
 * moving like everything else. An out-of-band terminator ends a name
 * inside the tree, so a NUL byte is an ordinary name character, as
 * @c cvn requires.
 *
 * Interning consults the local bank first, then the global, and the
 * order is load-bearing: the same spelling may be interned in both
 * banks, and the two are different objects with different indices --
 * different dictionary keys. System names are interned globally at
 * boot (the operator table records its names by global index); a name a program makes goes wherever the
 * VM mode says. A name once interned is never given back: names are
 * identities, and the cost of keeping them is the string and a
 * node per character, once.
 *
 * The name-not-found answer is itself a name: index zero of the
 * local stack is a distinguished bogus name, so the machinery can
 * pass "no such name" through channels that carry names without a
 * side channel, and a correct interpreter never shows it to a
 * program.
 *
 * A lookup that misses both trees -- the first interning -- copies
 * the characters out of virtual memory before inserting, because
 * insertion allocates and the characters being interned may
 * themselves live in the arena that allocation may move. The search
 * itself allocates nothing and reads the caller's bytes in place.
 *
 * @section design_dicts Dictionaries
 *
 * A dictionary is an open-hash table over key/value record pairs,
 * sized to an odd count with at least one slot always empty so a
 * probe can terminate on null. The internal table is allocated a
 * quarter larger than the requested capacity and grows by doubling,
 * so the load factor stays under a half. Three lengths are kept
 * deliberately distinct: @c length is the pairs in use, @c maxlength
 * is the capacity the program asked for, and the internal size is
 * neither -- it is what full-and-grow decisions read.
 *
 * Keys hash over exactly the fields their own type occupies -- a
 * composite over its entity, size and offset; a number over its
 * bits; a name over its index and bank -- multiplied out by the
 * golden-ratio constant. Hashing the whole object regardless of type
 * is the classic mistake this avoids: fields a type does not use are
 * padding, and padding hashes forty thousand distinct numbers into
 * seventeen buckets. Probing is linear with an inlined name
 * comparison, names being most keys.
 *
 * Growth does not move the dictionary the program holds: a new table
 * is built at twice the capacity, every pair re-entered, and then the
 * two entities exchange storage, so every existing reference to the
 * dictionary now reaches the bigger table. Deletion re-slots the
 * probe cluster following the hole (Knuth's Algorithm R), so nothing
 * becomes unreachable behind it. Both writes run the copy-on-write
 * check first, which is the whole of the dictionary's part in save
 * and restore.
 *
 * @section design_save Save and restore
 *
 * @c save is copy-on-write. The save stack holds records pairing a
 * source entity with its copy; the first write into an array or
 * dictionary above the current save level copies it once, and
 * @c restore exchanges the two addresses in the table -- one exchange
 * per touched entity, no pointer chasing, nothing at all for what was
 * not written. Global memory is exempt (PLRM 3.7.3): what a program
 * puts there survives restore and is reclaimed by collection alone,
 * which is why the collector covers both banks.
 *
 * Two refinements keep restore proportional to what it must actually
 * do. The record stacks that back each save level are pooled per
 * level rather than allocated per save, so a job that saves and
 * restores per request does not spend a stack's worth of memory every
 * time round. And the sweep that closes files opened since the save
 * -- required by the language -- is driven by a census rather than a
 * walk: each bank keeps a count of live file entities by birth stamp
 * (the save depth at their creation, kept in the file row's mark
 * field, which files, exempt from copy-on-write, do not otherwise
 * use), so a restore knows without walking the table whether any file
 * was born above the restored depth, and the walk runs only when it
 * will find something.
 *
 * The boundary between jobs (@ref design_jobs) is not one of these
 * copy-on-write brackets: it reverts a whole-VM image to a fixed
 * baseline rather than replaying a save level, so it is total and
 * cannot fail part way. Program-level @c save and @c restore are the
 * copy-on-write above; the job boundary is the image restore. An
 * embedder can disable the encapsulation for a context whose jobs are
 * meant to share state.
 *
 * @section design_gc Garbage collection
 *
 * The collector is mark-sweep over the memory files, with the root
 * set enumerated bank by bank in @ref xpost_roots -- the save stack of each
 * bank, the stacks of every context sharing it, and the special
 * entities. The mark phase walks an explicit worklist rather than the
 * C stack, so a deep object graph costs queue entries rather than
 * stack frames.
 *
 * Marking covers the banks the sweep will read, no more. A collection
 * of both banks walks across the boundary, because an object in one
 * may be named from the other. A collection of local memory alone
 * stays in the local bank and stops at any reference that leaves it:
 * what lies beyond is not swept, and local may name global freely, so
 * crossing would walk the whole global graph -- some fifteen thousand
 * composites of frozen library, against a few hundred live local
 * objects -- to protect storage the sweep never touches. The
 * references the other way are enumerable: a global container may
 * hold no reference into local memory except the local dictionaries
 * systemdict names through sanctioned windows, and those are marked
 * as anchors whichever way the walk is asked for. The register of
 * windows is held to the code, and each anchored name to survival of
 * a local-only collection, by tests/check-global-local-anchors.sh.
 *
 * Collections are paced two ways, both cheap to ask. Bytes: an
 * allocation debit against a threshold
 * (XPOST_GARBAGE_COLLECTION_THRESHOLD, four megabytes by default -- a
 * measured value: wall time is flat from about a megabyte up, and a
 * much larger threshold buys no time while holding several times the
 * memory; XPOST_GC_THRESHOLD and @c setvmthreshold override it).
 * Entity numbers: past a table-size watermark, a
 * per-allocation budget is spent so a job that churns rows without
 * churning many bytes still collects before the table saturates.
 * Either way the allocator only marks a collection pending; it is
 * taken at the interpreter's safe point between operators.
 *
 * Neither pacing reaches a host resource an unreachable object holds,
 * and one of those runs out on its own account: the descriptor behind
 * a file object. An open spends almost no VM, so a job that opens
 * files and drops them reaches the descriptor limit long before either
 * threshold is crossed. That refusal asks for a collection itself and
 * answers @c collectretry, which puts the operator and its operands
 * back the way a blocked read is put back and runs it again after the
 * safe point has taken the collection. One retry is offered per
 * refusal, so a program really holding that many files open is told
 * @c limitcheck as the language has it (PLRM 8.2, @c file). The
 * collector carries its own diagnostics -- an independent
 * reachability verifier, a cross-bank scan and a census, switched on
 * by environment variables -- which the structural tests run and
 * require silent.
 *
 * @section design_free The free list
 *
 * Storage a collection recovers goes on free lists bucketed by size
 * class: sixteen buckets (XPOST_FREE_NBUCKETS), each covering a
 * doubling of sizes, heads kept in the FREE special entity's data
 * area. Freeing pushes in
 * constant time. An allocation searches its own class best-fit but
 * examines at most eight entries there (XPOST_FREE_SCAN_LIMIT) before
 * taking a guaranteed fit from a higher bucket or fresh space -- the bound matters:
 * without one the cost of allocating is proportional to everything
 * the job has ever released, a quadratic product on churny jobs, and
 * with it an allocation's search is constant. An oversized block serves a smaller request by
 * carving the tail off rather than carrying the slack for the life
 * of the allocation.
 *
 * The links live in the entity rows, not in the freed bytes. The
 * storage a freed entity stands for holds nothing the allocator
 * reads, so a stale write into freed storage can spoil no link, a
 * sanitizer can be told the whole extent is off limits rather than
 * all but its first word, and pages lying entirely inside free
 * blocks can be given back to the operating system without taking
 * the chain with them. A corrupt node found during the search --
 * a number out of range, a row that is not free -- empties all
 * sixteen heads and asks for a collection to rebuild them, rather
 * than following the corruption.
 *
 * @section design_return Compaction, and giving storage back
 *
 * The immediate forms of @c vmreclaim collect and then compact. An
 * operand of 1 collects local memory at once and marks the local
 * arena for compaction; 2 collects both banks and marks both arenas
 * (the negative operands and zero only govern which banks automatic
 * collection may sweep, per the language). Compaction itself runs at
 * the interpreter's next safe point, not inside the operator: the
 * live entities are sorted by address and slid
 * down over the free blocks between them, so the free storage
 * gathers into one run at the top, and the cursor comes back to
 * where live storage ends. This is writable at all only because
 * every block in the arena carries a table row -- a block without
 * one could not be seen and would be slid over -- and the one field
 * that says where an entity's bytes are is the row's, so moving an
 * entity is rewriting one field as the bytes move.
 *
 * The blocks slid over stop existing: the lists that named them are
 * emptied, and their rows -- rows now describing nothing -- go on a
 * list of their own to be reissued with fresh storage. Without that
 * second list a job that fragments and compacts repeatedly would
 * spend the whole range of entity numbers without spending any
 * memory.
 *
 * Then the pages under the freed run go back to the operating system
 * in a single call, each host by its own route:
 *
 *   Linux          madvise MADV_DONTNEED
 *   Windows        DiscardVirtualMemory
 *   other POSIX    the range re-mapped in place, MAP_FIXED
 *
 * The portable route serves Linux too at about three times the cost
 * of the native call, and XPOST_RETURN_REMAPS asks for it anyway,
 * which is how it is exercised on hosts that would not otherwise
 * take it. All three leave the range writable, so nothing need be
 * asked before the storage is used again; what a discarded page
 * costs is the fault to fill it back in, paid by whichever later
 * allocation touches it. Storage is returned a page at a time,
 * which is not the grain the region is handed out in (on Windows the
 * two differ sixteenfold, the region being handed out in
 * allocation-granularity units), and the two grains are kept as
 * separate numbers -- @c xpost_memory_page_size and
 * @c xpost_memory_return_grain.
 *
 * Handing a page back changes what a bank holds without anything having
 * written it, so a boundary that puts back only the pages a job wrote
 * would leave this storage as the system left it. The range given back
 * is remembered for that boundary; see @ref design_jobs.
 *
 * Compaction happens only at the interpreter's outermost loop, never
 * inside the operator that asked: moving the arena invalidates every
 * pointer derived from an entity's address, an operator runs
 * underneath machinery holding such pointers, and so does every
 * nested procedure run, each holding the storage of the array it is
 * stepping. Only the outermost loop has none of those below it, and
 * tests/check-compaction-safe-point.sh holds the code to that. A
 * reclaim asked deep inside a procedure waits for the procedure, and
 * loses nothing by waiting: the program asked for a reclaim, not for
 * a moment.
 *
 * After compaction @c vmstatus reports where live storage ends. In
 * ordinary running its @c used figure is a cursor that only rises --
 * reuse from the free lists does not move it -- so it equals live
 * bytes only immediately after a compaction. The trade is stated
 * rather than hidden: compaction costs moving every live byte now
 * and faulting pages back later, and a program asking for it is
 * saying it would rather pay that than hold the memory.
 *
 * The page return has a second caller: the job boundary
 * (@ref design_jobs). No compaction runs there -- the revert has
 * already brought the cursor down to the baseline, so the arena above
 * it is the growth the finished job forced and is nobody's -- and the
 * whole run above the cursor goes back in the one call, where
 * compaction returns a gathered gap below it. The call and its two
 * grains are the same either way; an idle server then holds the
 * baseline arena and not the peak a passing job reached.
 *
 * @section design_loop The interpreter loop
 *
 * The outermost loop runs one executable object per turn, and
 * between turns does the work that must not happen inside one: take
 * a pending collection (reading and clearing the pending flag of
 * both banks -- a job may allocate in either), take a pending
 * compaction, surface a refused stack push as the @c VMerror it is,
 * hear an interrupt raised from a signal handler, and every
 * thousand or so turns check the stacks against their ceilings so a
 * runaway recursion is reported once rather than per turn.
 *
 * Dispatch by type is a direct switch -- operators to the operator
 * dispatcher, procedures to the procedure walker, names to
 * resolution, literals pushed -- because a switch predicts where an
 * indirect jump through a table does not; the table survives for the
 * rare types.
 *
 * Name resolution goes through a per-context cache keyed by name and
 * bank, validated by a generation counter that every operation
 * changing what a name can resolve to bumps: dictionary puts and
 * undefs, begin and end, restore, and clearing the dictionary
 * stack. A hit costs an array read and a generation compare; a miss
 * walks the dictionary stack segments topmost-first and stamps the
 * answer. A @c def into the topmost dictionary rewrites the one
 * cache entry in place instead of invalidating the world, since its
 * effect on the visible binding is deterministic. When a collection
 * of one bank runs, only entries whose value lives in the collected
 * bank are retired -- for a local collection the frozen global
 * library, which is most of what resolutions land on, stays cached;
 * this matters to a serving process that collects at every job,
 * which otherwise refilled the cache with a dictionary-stack walk
 * per name afterwards.
 *
 * The procedure walker is where the interpreter spends its life, and
 * it is aggressively flattened. Stepping a procedure resolves the
 * array's storage and the stack segment pointers once and steps
 * elements in a C loop rather than re-entering the interpreter per
 * element. Literals go straight to the operand stack. A name
 * resolving through the cache to another procedure is stepped in
 * place -- a procedure call without surfacing -- and @c if and
 * @c ifelse continue into the chosen branch in the same frame.
 * Twenty operators are executed inline when their operands sit in
 * the operand stack's top segment: the stack shufflers (pop, exch,
 * dup, index, roll), integer add, sub and mul with overflow falling
 * back to the general case, the six relationals, the conditionals,
 * def, array get and put, and type. Every fused body reuses the
 * operator's own shared core -- the same access checks, the same
 * range-preserving arithmetic, the same comparison -- and any
 * precondition failure falls through to the generic path, keeping
 * error behaviour identical; a structural test extracts the fused
 * list from the source and requires an equivalence case for each, so an
 * operator cannot join the fast path without joining the proof. The
 * walker re-derives its cached pointers whenever either arena has moved,
 * roots the element being executed so an anonymous procedure is not
 * swept mid-element, and repeats
 * the collection safe point and the push-refusal check inside the
 * loop so a long fused stretch starves neither.
 *
 * Operator dispatch matches the operand stack against up to eight
 * typed signatures per operator: an arity check, then per-operand
 * type patterns (exact, any, number, float -- promoting integers to
 * reals in place and rolling the promotion back if no signature
 * matches -- and procedure). The matched signature's C function is
 * called with the operands as ordinary arguments, moved as a block
 * into the single-segment hold stack and cracked as an array. Two
 * fast paths carry most traffic: a lone zero-operand C signature is
 * called directly, skipping the counting and the move (nearly half
 * the calls a rendering job makes are the looping operators'
 * rescheduled step operators, which are exactly this shape), and
 * the operand count is gauged from the top segment rather than by
 * walking the whole stack, so the guard on every call is constant
 * rather than O(depth). Operators implemented in PostScript occupy the
 * same table and state typed signatures exactly as compiled ones do
 * -- including up to eight alternative operand shapes, tried in
 * stated order -- so the dispatcher enforces the statement before
 * the body runs; the body itself runs through a bracketed frame on
 * the execution stack, so to a program they are operators -- the
 * self-redefinition idioms work -- while to the machinery they are
 * procedures.
 *
 * @section design_scanner The scanner
 *
 * The scanner is an operator (@c token) like any other, and reads
 * the full lexical language: radix numbers in bases 2 to 36 with
 * overflow detected rather than wrapped, ASCII base-85 string
 * literals with their five-to-four packing, and the binary token
 * encodings -- fixed-format objects introduced by bytes 128 through
 * 159 where a token is expected, covering binary object sequences,
 * both byte orders of integers and fixed-point numbers, three
 * string forms, homogeneous number arrays, and names by index into
 * the standard system name table. The scanner's buffer is sized to
 * the longest legal string literal a 16-bit length field can name,
 * which base-85 z-runs can reach from modest input. Its conformance
 * suite is adjudicated against two independent implementations,
 * with the differences recorded.
 *
 * @section design_errors Errors
 *
 * The error mechanism bridges the two languages. The C side raises:
 * an operator that refuses returns an error code, and the
 * interpreter's error path unwinds the stacks to their depths at the
 * innermost live wrapped call -- an operator leaves nothing behind
 * when it refuses, neither the values its own body pushed nor the
 * machinery's structures -- before anything is recorded. The
 * PostScript side reports: err.ps installs the standard @c $error
 * dictionary and the per-error handlers, and the handler snapshots
 * the operand, execution and dictionary stacks into @c $error (the
 * snapshot can be declined by clearing @c recordstacks), records the
 * error's name and the offending command, and either prints the
 * familiar banner or, when asked, emits the report as a binary
 * object sequence. The program-visible error state lives in
 * userdict, where the language's published shape puts it, while the
 * machinery keeps its own reference beside it -- so a program may
 * inspect, replace or extend error handling without being able to
 * starve the machinery of it.
 *
 * Two guards keep failure failing safely. An error raised while an
 * error is being reported is allowed -- handlers are programs too --
 * but a cascade is bounded (four thousand and ninety-six deep)
 * before the interpreter concludes the error machinery itself is
 * broken and stops, so a corrupted handler cannot loop the process
 * forever. And an interrupt is a flag raised from a signal handler
 * -- one volatile word, nothing else touched in signal context --
 * which the outermost loop turns into the language's own
 * @c interrupt error at the next safe point, so an interrupt lands
 * between operators like every other intervention, never inside
 * one.
 *
 * @section design_jobs The job layer and the interactive top level
 *
 * Above the interpreter proper sits a small job layer, part in
 * PostScript and part in C. Each job is encapsulated (PLRM 3.7.7):
 * when a job's execution is over, the context reverts to a fixed
 * baseline -- both virtual memory banks and the interpreter state
 * that lives outside them -- so nothing one job did reaches the next.
 * The revert is a whole-VM image restore rather than a save/restore
 * of the job's own: it puts each bank's baseline image back and moves
 * the bank's cursor to where it stood, which is total (strings and
 * stack contents revert with the objects), cannot fail part way (it
 * allocates nothing), and leaves no per-job garbage (the cursor
 * returns to the baseline), so one context can serve job after job
 * with encapsulation left on.
 *
 * Putting a baseline back does not mean copying all of it. A job commonly
 * writes a small fraction of the bank the boundary restores, and copying
 * the rest over itself is memory bandwidth, which is the one resource
 * concurrent renders do not each bring more of. So where the host can say
 * which pages a job wrote, the boundary puts back only those, and the
 * first boundary of a context
 * copies and arranges that for the ones after it. Each host is asked in
 * its own way and none of that reaches the memory file, which says only
 * what it wants; @c xpost_vm_writeset.h holds the three answers and what
 * each was measured to cost. The properties above are unchanged, because
 * copying the whole baseline remains the answer wherever a host has none:
 * the revert is still total, and still cannot fail part way, and
 * XPOST_NO_REVERT_WRITTEN takes every host down that road, which is how
 * the two are compared on a machine that is not this one.
 *
 * Putting a written page back is not necessarily copying over it. Where
 * the bank is a private view of the baseline, the page is discarded
 * instead, and the baseline shows through again without any of it being
 * moved. The difference is not speed but whether the storage ever comes
 * back: a page copied over stays a private copy, so a bank that copied
 * would hold every page it had ever written for as long as the run
 * lasted, while discarding leaves it holding what one job wrote.
 *
 * Two things the record of writes cannot see are put back beside it. A
 * page the arena handed to the operating system during the job -- the
 * return described in @ref design_return -- changed what the bank holds
 * with nothing having written it, so the range given back is remembered
 * and restored with the rest; without that the boundary would put back
 * what the job wrote and leave this storage as the system left it.
 * And a host that named too few pages would leave a job's bytes in the
 * next job's memory, which is a wrong answer no other check would see,
 * so XPOST_REVERT_VERIFY holds the bank against the baseline it has just
 * been said to hold and says which byte first differs.
 *
 * The bank's committed pages come back
 * with its cursor: the growth a large job forced on the arena is
 * returned to the operating system at the boundary -- the page return
 * of @ref design_return -- and the arena's own bound comes down with
 * the cursor, so @c globalvmstatus reports the arena a job begins
 * from and a server's footprint tracks the job it is running rather
 * than the largest it has ever run. The baseline is the initial VM a job
 * begins from -- the loaded language, a made device, and any
 * server-level prelude -- and @c exitserver and @c startjob fold a
 * run's definitions into it, so those definitions outlive the job.
 * An embedder can decline the encapsulation for a context whose jobs
 * share state. A job that ends in an error is routed through one
 * handler that reports and cleans up, so a failed job leaves the next
 * a clean interpreter.
 *
 * Putting the two banks back is not the whole of the revert. A few
 * fields of the context name entities inside the arena from outside
 * it -- the current page device and the procedure that retires it,
 * the window a device draws into and its event handler, the two font
 * directories, the object a run is executing -- and a field left
 * naming an entity the revert has dropped is a dangling reference the
 * next collection would follow into storage since handed to another
 * object. So these are put back to the baseline with the banks, from
 * the one list that declares them as the collector's roots; only the
 * held job-stream channel is exempt, because it lives in C and the
 * job layer opens and closes it around the boundary itself. The same
 * reasoning reaches two caches of interned names the context keeps
 * beside those roots -- the name a wrapped call's saved operands are
 * kept under, and the executable name the @c type operator answers for
 * each type. A name object is an index into a name stack that lives in
 * virtual memory, so a name a job interned is dropped by the revert
 * while the field caching it, being no part of the arena, keeps the
 * index; both are put back with the banks they index into.
 *
 * @subsection design_jobs_contract What a job may and may not rely on
 *
 * The isolation contract is what an embedder serving untrusted jobs
 * from one context is buying, so it is worth stating in full. With
 * encapsulation on -- the default -- a job may rely on beginning from
 * the baseline in every one of these:
 *
 * - Both virtual memory banks, whole. Every dictionary, every string,
 *   every array, every save level, the operand, dictionary, execution
 *   and graphics state stacks, and so the graphics state itself: the
 *   current transformation, colour space, colour, clip, font,
 *   halftone, transfer function, flatness and line parameters. The
 *   resource categories and everything defined into one, the font
 *   directories, @c $error and @c errordict, @c statusdict and
 *   @c serverdict, and any redefinition of a standard operator a job
 *   made anywhere it could reach. PLRM 3.7.7 asks for an empty operand
 *   stack, the standard dictionary stack, and stacks with no vestiges
 *   of previous jobs, and the image restore gives all of it at once.
 * - The operator table, and the count of operators issued, so an
 *   operator a job defined is neither callable nor counted in the next.
 * - The interpreter parameters that live outside the arena: the
 *   allocation mode, the array-packing mode, the random number
 *   generator's state, @c VMThreshold, @c IdiomRecognition, and the
 *   glyph cache's two byte parameters -- the @c MaxFontCache system
 *   parameter and the @c MaxFontItem user parameter of PLRM 8.2
 *   @c setcacheparams. PLRM C.1.1 has an encapsulated job's change to a
 *   user parameter leave the value later jobs begin from alone, and
 *   these are the ones the arena revert does not reach.
 * - The error latches the interpreter holds outside the arena: the
 *   flag per stack that says its overflow has been reported, and the
 *   count of errors handled without the run reaching @c stop, which is
 *   what bounds a cascade. The name and detail of the error a job ended
 *   on are the run's verdict rather than interpreter state -- the
 *   embedder reads them after the call -- and are cleared as the next
 *   job is primed.
 * - The files and filters the job opened, which are closed, and the
 *   page device it installed, which is retired -- its @c Destroy run
 *   while virtual memory is intact, as PLRM 6.1 has it, since only the
 *   @c Destroy frees the raster the device holds outside the arena.
 * - The blocks a job's entities named outside the arena. A handle is a
 *   record kept beside virtual memory, given up wherever the entity
 *   carrying it goes; the revert takes the whole arena back without
 *   walking an entity, so the boundary sweeps for the records the
 *   revert has stopped naming.
 * - The committed pages the job's growth cost, which go back to the
 *   operating system.
 *
 * What a job may @b not rely on, and what it therefore cannot use to
 * reach the job after it:
 *
 * - The counters that name cached work -- the serials a font, a form
 *   and a device region are keyed by. These are the interpreter's own
 *   and only ever move forward, deliberately: they key caches that are
 *   not virtual memory and are not wound back with it, so a counter
 *   that wound back would hand a later job a number an earlier job's
 *   cached work is still filed under. A job cannot make the interpreter
 *   reissue one.
 * - The glyph cache's contents and the faces the font library holds
 *   open. These are a cache in the strict sense: what they hold is
 *   keyed by the full rendering inputs, so a hit is the bytes a fresh
 *   rendering would produce and no job can be answered another job's
 *   work. Keeping them across the boundary is what a font cache is for,
 *   and PLRM 8.2 @c cachestatus reports figures a program cannot
 *   control directly.
 * - The path permits. The sandbox is the process's and is one-way by
 *   contract (@c xpost.h): a job neither widens it nor narrows it.
 *
 * And what persists on purpose: @c exitserver and @c true @c password
 * @c startjob make a run unencapsulated, and at its end the boundary
 * folds its state into the baseline instead of reverting to it, so its
 * definitions are part of the initial VM every later job begins from
 * (PLRM 3.7.7). @c false @c password @c startjob returns the run to
 * encapsulation, and the fold it would need is not taken where it
 * stands, because the boundary is the run's own end: a prolog opened by
 * @c true @c password @c startjob and closed by @c false @c password
 * @c startjob within one run does not reach the baseline, the run
 * ending encapsulated and reverting whole. PLRM 3.7.7 gives that very
 * pairing as the prolog and script idiom, so this is a deviation, and
 * it is the price of the boundary being an image restore: folding where
 * @c startjob stands would capture the execution stack in mid-flight,
 * and a later revert to that image would resume a stale continuation. A
 * prolog that must outlive its run is left unencapsulated to the end of
 * it, or installed through @c xpost_job_baseline_set.
 * The door is a password: empty, the factory default,
 * lets a trusted prolog through, and a host serving untrusted jobs
 * closes it with @c xpost_startjob_password_set, since a program cannot
 * set it.
 *
 * A run's input may itself be a job-server channel carrying more than
 * one job, framed by the Control-D end-of-file the serial protocol
 * uses (PLRM 3.7.7): the interpreter takes the boundary at each
 * delimiter and reads the next job from the same stream, all within
 * one call -- the stream is held in C, outside the virtual memory the
 * boundary reverts, which is what lets a boundary be taken in
 * mid-run. Control-D belongs to that channel and not to the language,
 * so it frames only a stream an embedder marks as one, never a file a
 * program opens or a nested read. The language-shaped
 * furniture lives where the published shape puts it: statusdict
 * (host and device status a program may change), serverdict with
 * @c exitserver (the escape from job encapsulation), and
 * UserObjects -- all userdict members by convention, and all local
 * dictionaries reached through systemdict by the machinery. An
 * interactive run adds the executive: a prompt loop that scans,
 * executes and reports per statement, layered on the same job
 * machinery rather than beside it. The quiet flag a run starts
 * with governs the boot narration and is part of what a VM image
 * carries, which is why an image and the run reading it must agree
 * about it.
 *
 * @section design_contexts Contexts, scheduling and embedding
 *
 * Execution contexts are threads of PostScript execution inside one
 * interpreter, held in its context table; they are not multiple
 * interpreters. The @c fork operator (installed on the @c --enable-dps
 * opt-in) shares both memory files with the child and gives it an
 * environment of its own: PLRM 2nd ed 7.1 makes a context private
 * except for VM, so the stacks, the dictionary and graphics state
 * stacks, and the current path are the child's, while VM -- and so
 * userdict and every composite -- is shared. Context identifiers are
 * issued so a slot can be reused while every identifier in the
 * process's history stays unique: they run from one, and a context's
 * seat in the table is @c (cid-1) modulo the table's size, so the
 * allocator steps the counter on until that lands on an empty seat. A
 * context-typed handle carries an identifier so a program cannot forge
 * one from a number.
 *
 * Scheduling is cooperative -- concurrency, not preemption. A context
 * runs until it yields, blocks on I/O, or returns from its top-level
 * procedure; at each of those the loop takes the next runnable context
 * from the table, wrapping, and a context waiting on a join or blocked
 * on I/O is made runnable again as it is passed so it re-checks its
 * condition. @c fork returns that verdict, so the child runs; @c join
 * collects the child's operand stack when it finishes and @c detach
 * lets it free itself instead of waiting. A forked procedure runs as
 * @c "proc stopped { handleerror quit } if", so a context that ends on
 * an error reports it and terminates, and a later join of it is
 * @c invalidcontext. Context validation happens exactly at a switch,
 * once per switch, rather than once per object executed. The whole
 * mechanism reads @c xpost_dps_enabled first, so a run that never
 * installed the operators keeps the single-context path it had.
 *
 * A read that would have to wait is where the blocking comes from, and
 * the read decides it rather than the scheduler. Asked to wait, it first
 * asks whether any other context could run: if one could, it polls
 * without waiting and answers @c ioblock, so the loop puts the operator
 * back -- operands out of the hold, the operator itself back on the
 * execution stack -- and gives the run to somebody else; the read
 * happens again from the top when this context is next chosen, having
 * consumed nothing. If none could, it waits in @c select() as it would
 * have anyway. Handing that decision to the scheduler instead is what
 * would break it: with one context the round-robin returns that same
 * context, the read retries at once, and a sleep becomes a spin. The
 * answer is offered for one plain host stream at a time and never for a
 * filter's source, an unexpected end-of-file being something a filter
 * would take for the end of its data.
 *
 * The same loop is the embedder's seam. A run can be created with
 * showpage semantics that suspend the program just before showpage
 * returns, handing control back to the caller with the page's
 * raster available; calling run again resumes the suspended program
 * for the next page. The interpreter can also run a nested
 * PostScript call from inside C -- the graphics machinery does this
 * constantly -- bounded at a stated depth and refused with
 * @c limitcheck past it. Every route by which the machinery calls
 * back into program code -- a pattern's paint procedure, a Type 3
 * build procedure, a tint transform, an image's data source or
 * decode, a spot function, a transfer function -- goes through one
 * shared bracket, so the bookkeeping that makes a callback safe
 * (what to restore, what errors mean, what the callback may see) is
 * written once rather than once per callback kind. The window
 * devices hang their event handling off the loop's idle hook, which
 * is also why a process runs at most one window device: the loop
 * has one seat for a handler needing constant attention.
 *
 * @section design_devices Devices
 *
 * A device is a dictionary of named methods -- a small systemdict
 * for marks. The raster formats are classes implemented in
 * PostScript over one shared method suite, specialised per format
 * by a class generator: grayscale and colour are parameterisations,
 * bilevel and TIFF are copies with emission overridden. A class can
 * be subclassed, overriding as much or as little as it needs. An
 * instance is a copy of its class with the page's dimensions and
 * the run's requests written over it.
 *
 * The suite is small enough to state. Taking the 8-bit grayscale class
 * as the example -- colour differs only in how many colour values each
 * procedure is handed:
 *
 * @verbatim
        width height CLASS  Create    ->  instance
                val x y DEV  PutPix    ->  -
                    x y DEV  GetPix    ->  val
        val x1 y1 x2 y2 DEV  DrawLine  ->  -
   val x y width height DEV  DrawRect  ->  -
   val x y width height DEV  FillRect  ->  -
            val polygon DEV  FillPoly  ->  -
                        DEV  Emit      ->  -
                        DEV  Destroy   ->  -
@endverbatim
 *
 * with the unoverridden implementations resolving downward: DrawLine
 * walks a Bresenham DDA through PutPix, and FillRect and FillPoly draw
 * a line per scanline. So a class that overrides nothing but PutPix is
 * already a working device, and one that overrides the span-level
 * methods is a fast one. Create builds the raster as @c /ImgData, an
 * array of one string per scanline, priced before any of it is built;
 * an embedder wanting the pixels can override @c Emit and read that
 * array. A device may also implement @c /Flush, which puts out whatever
 * it has buffered so the output keeps step with the program.
 *
 * The compiled drivers -- PNG, JPEG, the embedder's raster and BGR
 * buffers, the window devices -- install C operators over the same
 * shape: each overrides the marking methods (pixel put and blend,
 * rectangle fill, and for the window devices line and polygon fill
 * too), Create, Emit and Destroy, and keeps the class's PostScript
 * for everything that runs once per page. A device that keeps a
 * buffer of its own rather than the class's array of rows must
 * override every inherited method that would reach for that array
 * -- including the band loop's row mover -- because the inherited
 * method would answer for rows the instance does not carry.
 * Declarations are restated rather than inherited where a copy
 * would otherwise carry its parent's answer: whether a page may
 * arrive in bands, and what a row costs, are stated by every class
 * for itself, and a structural test holds every class to having
 * said something.
 *
 * A compiled device's C-side state -- its raster, a writer's
 * accumulated content -- lives outside virtual memory, and what the
 * instance dictionary holds under /Private is a handle: a number
 * issued by a registry that records which block, of what kind and
 * size, was issued to which dictionary. Resolution is checked at
 * every step -- a handle naming no live block, a block of another
 * kind or size, or a handle copied into a dictionary it was not
 * issued to resolves to nothing and the reader reports rather than
 * follows -- so a program that forges or moves the number gets a
 * refusal, never an address. Each block carries its release with it
 * from the moment it is issued, and is given back when the entity
 * carrying the handle is reclaimed, from whichever of the three
 * routes reaches it first (the sweep, an explicit free, teardown).
 * The window drivers load in two halves for a reason worth stating,
 * because it is the general shape of a compiled device built on a
 * PostScript one: loading installs a maker, and the maker's Create runs
 * the base class's Create -- which may itself be PostScript -- before
 * overriding the drawing methods on the instance it gets back. C cannot
 * call into the interpreter and carry on down the same C frame, so each
 * half ends where the interpreter takes over and the rest is a
 * continuation the interpreter calls next.
 *
 * No raw pointer is ever held in a
 * device dictionary, and the same property is what makes VM images
 * refusable: an image is declined at the write if any entity carries
 * a handle, because a handle stands for nothing in another process.
 *
 * Configuration flows one way. A device's tunable features are
 * device parameters set through @c setpagedevice -- unknown keys
 * and out-of-range values are refused loudly, against a roster each
 * driver states beside the code that reads the knob -- or from the
 * command line with @c -p, or by an embedder before any run. The
 * early settings land in the host dictionary and are copied onto a
 * driver's class as the class is built, so the call means the same
 * thing whenever it is made; a page-device request always wins,
 * because its keys are written over the class's entries as the
 * instance is made; and one pending request is armed at a time, so
 * a driver reload cannot take a request armed for another device.
 * The roster is gathered from what this build compiled in, so a
 * knob belonging to an omitted driver is not offered. Which devices
 * exist, and what their selection strings mean, is doc/MANUAL's
 * table -- machine-read by a test, so the roster cannot drift from
 * the drivers.
 *
 * @section design_imaging The imaging pipeline
 *
 * Painting resolves marks into device space above the device, so
 * every device -- compiled or PostScript, painting or recording --
 * is asked the same small questions: put or blend a pixel, fill a
 * rectangle, fill a polygon's spans. The span machinery is
 * compiled: edges are sorted with the comparison written in, a
 * fill's cost is proportional to total edge height rather than
 * edges times page height, and the settled spans go to the device
 * through the compiled rectangle fill without an interpreted loop
 * between them.
 *
 * Images take one of two routes. Where the mapping is axis-aligned,
 * the clip is rectangular and the device keeps rows, each image row
 * is written through one compiled row blitter, with the colour
 * pipeline baked ahead of the loop: a one-component space bakes
 * decode, conversion and transfer into a single 256-entry table by
 * running each possible sample through the interpreter's own
 * machinery once; multi-component spaces take per-component decode
 * tables and apply transfer after conversion. The blitter resolves,
 * per sample and per device row, the run of device columns that
 * sample covers, and states the run either into the device's rows
 * or -- for a device keeping its own buffer -- as one call to its
 * compiled rectangle fill; where a mask on a grid finer than the
 * image's steps across the sample, what is stated is the runs that
 * mask leaves rather than the one the sample covers. Masks arrive
 * as bits over a grid of their own, or as ranges, saying which
 * pixels to leave unpainted. Everything else -- rotated images,
 * exotic clips, devices without rows -- takes the general route,
 * one rectangle per sample, through the same operators as any
 * other mark. The two routes end at the same two sinks, which
 * is what keeps a page painted directly and a page replayed from a
 * record byte-identical.
 *
 * Halftoning asks one compiled question -- ink or no ink for this
 * device pixel at this coverage -- from one site, with the
 * threshold cell's geometry derived once and its names resolved
 * once rather than per span.
 *
 * @section design_colour Colour, patterns, shadings, halftones
 *
 * Colour spaces are implemented as a family in the PostScript
 * graphics layer: the device spaces, the CIE-based spaces, indexed
 * and separation spaces, each knowing its component count, its
 * reduction to the device's native space, and its role on the image
 * path. Setting a colour reduces it through one walk of that
 * pipeline rather than one walk per question asked later; on the
 * image fast path the whole pipeline -- decode, conversion,
 * transfer -- is baked into lookup tables before the first sample,
 * which is what lets a million samples pay the pipeline's cost
 * once. The family's shape is held by a facts register: every
 * space's declared properties are compared against the
 * implementation by a gate, in both directions, so a space cannot
 * be added or changed without the register saying so.
 *
 * Patterns paint their cells by running the pattern's paint
 * procedure once per cell -- program code, through the shared
 * callback bracket -- with the cell's marks going to the device
 * like any others (and into the record, when the page is recorded,
 * once per cell: a pattern's cells fall under no bitmap-sharing
 * rule, because each cell is a fresh run of a program). Shadings
 * subdivide their geometry to a stated target -- half a device unit,
 * with a cap on the halvings -- so their smoothness is a function of
 * the device resolution rather than a constant chosen once.
 * Halftone screens are threshold cells derived once per screen
 * change: the cell's geometry is computed at one site whatever
 * shape described it, the per-pixel question is asked at one
 * compiled site, and a screen in force is state a record carries
 * across band and page boundaries rather than a mark inside them.
 *
 * @section design_vector The vector writers
 *
 * Three devices write structure rather than pixels: pdfwrite,
 * svgwrite and dscwrite. Each is a PostScript class implementing
 * the same driver contract as the rasters -- they receive the same
 * resolved marks, which is what keeps their output geometrically
 * identical to the rendered page -- and each holds its page whole:
 * banding is a raster economy, and a writer's page is text. The
 * document being accumulated lives outside virtual memory, in
 * blocks named by content handles, for the same reason a raster
 * does: a restore must not tear a half-written document, and
 * @c vmstatus should not bill the program for the writer's own
 * buffers. The per-byte work is compiled -- flate compression and
 * base64 encoding are C helpers -- while the structure (objects and
 * cross-reference tables, elements and attributes, structuring
 * comments) is PostScript. The writers' process-wide bookkeeping is
 * allocated once per process rather than per document, so a process
 * serving many documents does not grow by a document's bookkeeping
 * per request. dscwrite emits the
 * structuring conventions the DSC library parses -- the two are the
 * write and read halves of the same convention, and deliberately do
 * not share code: a writer that read its own output would only
 * prove it agrees with itself.
 *
 * A fourth structural device, bbox, renders nothing and reports the
 * bounding box of what would have been painted. It declares the
 * fill-path method so it reads extents off the path itself --
 * exact, and independent of the device resolution -- rather than
 * accumulating the marks' own bounds.
 *
 * @section design_banding The recorded page, and banding by default
 *
 * A raster costs width times height whatever is painted on it.
 * xpost's answer is to hold a page as the marks that made it -- a
 * record -- and to play those marks into the device a band of rows
 * at a time, so the pixel storage for a page is bounded by a budget
 * rather than by the page.
 *
 * The record lives outside virtual memory: @c restore does not
 * reach it and @c vmstatus does not count it. The recording device
 * implements the driver contract but, instead of painting, writes
 * down what it was asked to paint. It declares exactly the five
 * marking methods it records -- pixel put and blend, line,
 * rectangle, polygon -- and no optional method, so everything above
 * the device resolves clips, outlines and page resets into those
 * five before they arrive; a recorder declaring more would put
 * marks beyond the record's reach. An entry is one of nine kinds:
 *
 * @verbatim
   PUTPIX    <colour> x y
   BLENDPIX  <colour> coverage x y
   DRAWLINE  <colour> x1 y1 x2 y2
   FILLRECT  <colour> x y w h
   FILLPOLY  <colour> n, then n pairs
   IMAGE     which picture, placed by its own transform
   SCREEN    which threshold cell
   GLYPH     which coverage mask, then x y
   PLACE     which drawing, then dx dy
@endverbatim
 *
 * Every entry carries the run of rows it can reach, and that range
 * must be a conservative superset: a visit too many costs time, while
 * a mark judged out of a band it touches is simply absent from the
 * page. A band replay visits only the entries whose range meets the
 * band. A record is fixed to the resolution it was made at -- its
 * coordinates are device space, the clip already resolved into them
 * above the device -- so replaying it at another scale would give a
 * scaled copy of the same pixels rather than a re-rendering.
 *
 * Bulk pixels follow one rule: a bitmap produced once is held once
 * and re-emitted per band. A picture is one entry naming its
 * samples, transform and decode; a glyph is one entry naming a
 * coverage mask, shared on its bytes; a screen cell is state, not a
 * mark -- played in order, exempt from the row filter, surviving
 * the page boundary. A recorder is not told when a page ends: it
 * reads the boundary off the marks (a page reset arrives as a
 * covering rectangle), which is what keeps @c copypage's
 * transmit-without-erase meaning what it says.
 *
 * Which classes will take a banded page is a declaration, and silence
 * means no. The PostScript raster formats declare it -- grayscale,
 * colour and the bilevel and TIFF copies of them -- and so do PNG and
 * JPEG. Three kinds of device take it back out: the embedder's raster
 * and BGR buffers, whose page is the caller's and whole by contract;
 * the window devices, whose drivers write the page whole; and every
 * compiled device keeping a raster of its own. The alpha PNG device
 * takes it back out for a reason of its own, and the reason is worth
 * stating because it is a limit of the mechanism rather than a
 * preference: a page arrives in bands by being recorded and played
 * back, a record writes a page reset down as a rectangle in the colour
 * the page clears to, and the page this device clears to is transparent
 * rather than any colour -- so the reset that makes it what it is does
 * not survive the round trip. Its writer takes a page a band at a time
 * regardless, which is exercised with no record in front of it.
 *
 * The declaration and the routing are a pair. A separate roster says
 * which devices a record may be played into, and a device that
 * declares a banded page while appearing in no route promises an
 * arrival no budget can give it. A guard holds the two together by
 * asking a running interpreter what each class declares, rather than
 * reading it off a driver's source: a class is built and not spelled,
 * and one driver body here makes two classes that answer differently.
 * What a row costs is declared the same way, and a copy must restate
 * it rather than inherit it -- a budget divided by a number nobody
 * restated buys a band of some other height than the one it paid for.
 *
 * Whether a page bands at all is one number against another. A
 * class declares what a row of its raster costs; a run has a byte
 * budget (four megabytes unless \--band-bytes names another); the
 * band height is the budget
 * over the row cost, and a page bands exactly when the budget buys
 * fewer rows than the page has, so it bands the moment it would need
 * a second band and never bands into one. The decision is taken where
 * a device is made, at the page that device is to hold -- for the
 * device a run opens with, and again for every page-device request
 * that makes another -- and never between two of them, because a page
 * description states its page by declaring it, and weighing only the
 * page a run opened at would hold every page a job asked for whole.
 *
 * At four megabytes the ordinary sheets are painted directly: letter
 * is 612 by 792 at three bytes a pixel, or 1,454,112 bytes; A4 is
 * 1,502,970 and A3 is 3,008,466. What the bound is for is untouched by
 * those -- a 612 by 40,000 strip costs 73,440,000 bytes held whole,
 * and is held in 2,178 rows of that instead. A run naming a budget
 * below the ordinary figures is what bands an ordinary sheet. The
 * comparison is a page's raster against the budget, so what holding
 * the marks instead costs is not in it: neither the record, nor the
 * objects a replay builds afresh for each band. The number was put
 * above the largest ordinary sheet's raster and rounded up; where the
 * two costs actually cross has not been measured, so it is a free
 * parameter with a reason for its size and no reading behind its
 * value.
 *
 * None of this is a page-device parameter, and none may become one:
 * PLRM Appendix G bars @c banddevice from a page description, so a
 * program may not choose how many times its marks are played. What
 * chooses is the invocation. Being told is the other half, and is
 * allowed: @c currentsystemparams reports the budget as
 * /MaxBandBytes and the rows it bought on the device now being
 * painted into as /CurBandHeight, which is 0 where that device holds
 * the page whole. @c setsystemparams recognises no parameter at all,
 * so neither can be written back. An /ImagingBBox declaration (PLRM 6.2, by which a
 * program asserts it paints nothing outside four numbers, and marks
 * that do "may or may not be rendered") narrows the page to the rows
 * it can reach, on both routes. It is not a clip: the page is the size
 * /PageSize named and the default clipping path is still the whole of
 * it. Whole rows are what a row-holding device can drop, so what a box
 * buys is the rows above its top edge and below its bottom edge, and
 * dropping them is the one answer of the two PLRM permits that both
 * routes can give. The run is computed once, where a device is about
 * to be made; the route is then weighed on the rows a device would
 * actually hold rather than on the page's height, so a box that brings
 * the raster under the budget hands the page to the device
 * directly.
 *
 * The band loop moves one raster rather than making one per band --
 * a retired device's rows are not given back, so a device per band
 * would end the loop holding the whole page. The rows array stays
 * the height of the page and the band's rows are the only ones with
 * width, so the row blitter needs no offset arithmetic and a
 * zero-width row is how a device says it holds no pixels there. What
 * that costs is one object slot per row of the page. What a device
 * must keep between bands cannot live in the rows, since those move,
 * so the file and how far down the page it has reached are kept
 * beside them; and a page goes out through three hooks rather than
 * one writer -- a head, a per-band sink and a tail -- with both routes
 * going through the same three, so the same bytes come out of one
 * statement rather than two that agree. A banding Emit is called once
 * per band with the rows the device holds at that moment, and once
 * more at the end with none, to say the page is finished.
 *
 * A band whose only mark is the page's own clearing colour, covering
 * it entirely, is not materialised at all. The test cannot be whether
 * anything reaches the band -- a recorder declares no Erase, so
 * clearing the page reaches it as a rectangle covering the page, and
 * every band of every page is reached by a mark. What is asked is
 * whether the last mark reaching the run is a rectangle, in the colour
 * the page was cleared to, covering every row of the run and the whole
 * width. What a skipped band saves is time and not memory: the raster
 * was priced and taken at Create.
 *
 * Replay is batched -- five hundred and twelve compiled marking calls
 * per turn of the interpreter (XPOST_REPLAY_BATCH), so collections and
 * interrupts still get their safe point -- and a method that is a
 * procedure rather than an operator is played by the interpreter
 * itself, at two turns per mark. A glyph mask is the one entry a batch
 * can end in the middle of, and it resumes at the pixel it stopped at.
 *
 * The correctness contract is byte identity: every gate that
 * compares pages renders the same page whole and in bands, down to
 * band heights of one row, and requires the outputs identical byte
 * for byte. Scan conversion is outside the language, so the
 * whole-page render of the same tree is the only oracle there is --
 * and the band route is held to it exactly. The cost is also
 * stated: a page that is almost entirely marks costs about twice as
 * much played in bands as painted directly, and a gate holds the
 * ratio at 23/10 -- a ratio, not a time, because a time would be a
 * statement about the machine the suite runs on.
 *
 * @section design_spill Where a record's marks are kept
 *
 * A record is held in memory while it is worth less than the raster
 * banding saves, and goes to a scratch file past that
 * (\--spill=auto|never|always). What the run asked for and where the
 * page in hand actually has its marks are reported separately, by
 * @c currentsystemparams as /RecordSpill and /CurRecordSpill, because
 * they can differ: a run that asked to be weighed, on a machine that
 * will take no scratch file, reports auto and refused. A record that
 * saves nothing is never spilled under auto -- it has no raster to be
 * worth more than. The weighing is O(1): a running
 * count of the record's blocks maintained as they are taken and
 * given up, asked every sixty-four marks (RECORD_WEIGH_EVERY) and at
 * any entry big enough to cross the threshold alone -- a picture, a
 * coverage mask, a polygon of more than sixty-four operands -- never a walk over the
 * masks and picture tables, which, asked per mark, would multiply
 * to masks-times-marks on a page of text.
 *
 * The scratch file has no name: created and unlinked in the same
 * breath (delete-on-close on Windows), it is unreachable through
 * the file machinery and the path sandbox has no path to confine.
 * Whether scratch space exists is settled by making the file and
 * writing to it when the device is made -- a permissions check
 * would answer yes for a full filesystem. One stream in mark order,
 * one write buffer and one read window (64 KiB each, _SPILL_BUFFER
 * and _SPILL_WINDOW): order is what
 * a record is for, and a chain per band would cost it. What one
 * stream costs is a pass over the file per band; a shallower band
 * is paid in reading rather than in memory. Everything the record
 * holds goes to the file -- samples, masks, cells -- and what stays
 * resident is the buffers, one small entry per distinct bulk item,
 * and the tables a picture is decoded through.
 *
 * Failure never yields a short page. A page missing a mark looks
 * like a page, so a record refused a mark -- a write that failed, a
 * read that came back short -- remembers, refuses every mark and
 * every replay after it, and reports @c ioerror rather than
 * @c VMerror: a caller told memory ran out when a filesystem filled
 * looks in the wrong place. A run under @c auto that cannot have a
 * scratch file is told once, naming the directory and the reason the
 * system gave, and goes on holding marks in memory; only @c always
 * makes no-scratch-space fatal. A scratch directory that becomes
 * unwritable after the file is open cannot reach the record at all:
 * the descriptor is open, the name went when the file was made, and
 * directory permissions are not consulted again.
 *
 * @section design_fonts Text
 *
 * Text runs through three kinds of font: host faces found by
 * fontconfig and rendered by FreeType, procedural (Type 3) fonts
 * described in PostScript, and Type 42 fonts carrying a TrueType
 * program. The machinery resolves its names once, as the operators
 * install, rather than per call: a show reaches the graphics state,
 * the font, the device and the colour through references resolved at
 * install time, where resolving them by name would cost tens of
 * dictionary walks per string painted.
 *
 * Three caches back the path. Faces: opening a face costs the file
 * and the derived objects, so faces are cached per font name, 32
 * entries, least-recently-used -- a name past the cache costs a
 * reopen, never a wrong font, and eviction cannot pull a face from
 * under a font that holds it, every font dictionary carrying its
 * own claim. Rendered glyphs: keyed by face, glyph, the exact
 * fixed-point transform and size, bounded in bytes and in count
 * (the language's cache parameters read and set the real bounds),
 * evicted least-recently-used; a glyph larger than the per-glyph
 * ceiling renders uncached rather than evicting the world. Type 3
 * glyphs: a build procedure's output is captured once as a coverage
 * mask through @c setcachedevice and re-placed thereafter, keyed by
 * a font serial that lives in C precisely so a @c restore cannot
 * wind it back while the masks it keys live on; the cache serves
 * only where placing the mask is exactly what painting would have
 * done -- within a clip that holds the whole raster, never under a
 * pattern -- and builds direct otherwise. Small stencil masks
 * additionally cache their resolved runs by content, because
 * driver-generated bitmap text paints the same few masks over and
 * over.
 *
 * The face library is optional by policy, not by accident: a build
 * without FreeType (-Dfreetype=disabled, or \--without-freetype) is
 * a font system with no faces, not one with a hole. Every font
 * operator exists; a name that needs a face is refused with
 * @c invalidfont at @c findfont, a font program that needs one at
 * @c definefont; and Type 3 fonts still define, still answer
 * @c findfont, and render byte-identically to the full build. The
 * faceless configuration cannot arrive silently -- a missing
 * library fails configuration unless the faceless build was asked
 * for by name -- and it runs in a CI lane of its own.
 *
 * @section design_resources Resources
 *
 * The resource machinery is the language's registry of named
 * instances per category, held in global memory. A category is two
 * things made together -- a table of its instances, and its
 * implementation dictionary, itself an instance of the /Category
 * category -- so defining an instance in /Category is what brings a
 * new category into existence, exactly as the language specifies
 * programs may. Fonts are the busiest category: findfont resolves
 * through the font directory and, for host faces, through
 * fontconfig's substitution -- with the steering recorded so the
 * suite can hold a name to the face it lands on -- and the
 * encodings are resources like any other.
 *
 * @section design_files Files and filters
 *
 * Files are objects over a small method table, and filters are
 * files layered on files, so everything that reads bytes reads them
 * one way. The stream decoders hold to a strict end-of-data
 * discipline -- a decoder consumes its marker at the read that
 * meets it -- so layered and consecutive filters stay in step on
 * the underlying stream. The method table carries an optional
 * block-write beside the byte operations: the disk stream writes a
 * device row as one call with one error poll, while filters keep
 * the byte path and its semantics. Restore's obligation to close
 * files created since the save is served by the birth-stamp census
 * (@ref design_save); a stream is bound to its entity by the same
 * handle registry as everything else outside VM, so a file entity
 * cannot be reclaimed out from under an open stream, and a freed
 * stream cannot be closed twice.
 *
 * @section design_forms Forms
 *
 * The language permits an interpreter to keep a form's output and
 * substitute it for re-execution. What is kept is a record of the
 * form's marks, not a raster: one execution serves every placement
 * under the same linear transformation wherever it falls -- the key
 * is the form and the linear part of the matrix, position
 * deliberately excluded -- and a use is one placement entry, so a
 * drawing placed twenty-five times costs one drawing, and nested
 * forms cost each distinct drawing once rather than their product.
 * Placement is bounded to a stated nesting depth (twelve,
 * XPOST_RECORD_NEST), refused with @c limitcheck past it -- at the
 * placement and again at the replay. A description is captured only where
 * placing the marks is exactly what painting them would have been:
 * nothing cut on the way in, every mark inside the box the form
 * declares -- the first counted as the description is captured, the
 * second read back off the record afterwards, and a form failing
 * either paints afresh at every use. The capture runs under a clip of
 * the declared box out to whole pixels and one further, which holds a
 * description that paints outside its declared box to about that box
 * and leaves one that keeps inside it uncut. The cache holds thirty-two drawings, evicting the
 * oldest capture and destroying its record explicitly, a record not
 * being anything virtual memory would give back.
 *
 * @section design_confinement Confinement and enforcement
 *
 * Three separable mechanisms, in order of strength. The path
 * sandbox confines what the file operators may open, and is on by
 * default. The access attributes of the language -- read-only,
 * execute-only, no-access -- are enforced across arrays, strings
 * and dictionaries, including on the interpreter's own fast paths,
 * so a program's barriers hold everywhere. And the interpreter's
 * internals are evacuated from the program-visible namespaces and
 * its cross-boundary references are checked numbers rather than
 * addresses -- entity numbers into tables, handles into a registry
 * that verifies kind, size and owner -- so the classic corruption
 * routes (a forged pointer in a device dictionary, a stale free, a
 * link written through freed storage) resolve to refusals. The
 * three are stated separately because they answer different
 * questions; none is a claim that a hostile program is harmless,
 * and the memory-safety work behind that question is ongoing and
 * test-held.
 *
 * @section design_dsc The DSC library
 *
 * Document Structuring Conventions -- the @c %% metadata comments a
 * conforming document carries -- are read by a second, independent
 * library, libxpost_dsc, which parses a document's header and page
 * table without executing it. The @c xpost_dsc program prints what
 * the comments say; the @c xpost_view viewer uses the per-page
 * offsets to render one page at a time. The interpreter itself
 * never parses DSC -- comments are comments -- while the two output
 * devices that emit DSC-conforming structure write it, which is the
 * other direction.
 *
 * @section design_testing The testing architecture
 *
 * The suite is part of the design, and its architecture is what the
 * rest of this page leans on when it says a rule is held. Around
 * four hundred registered tests run at both object widths, in
 * profiles from a fast gate to the full slow suite, and they divide
 * into kinds with different jobs.
 *
 * Behavioural tests state what the language does, and never lock in
 * a known deviation as if it were right: a deviation is recorded as
 * a deviation, so conformance work burns down a list rather than
 * fighting the suite. Byte goldens hold whole outputs -- rendered
 * pages, error transcripts, listings -- so a refactor that changes
 * nothing observable can prove it changed nothing observable, and
 * the band-versus-whole identity is checked byte for byte down to
 * one-row bands.
 *
 * Structural guards hold this page's kind of claim: a register of
 * facts (which devices exist, what each declares, which colour
 * spaces there are, what a workload's second run may cost, which
 * windows the memory barrier opens) is compared against the tree in
 * both directions, derived from the code by mechanism rather than
 * from a list kept beside it, so the state a new thing arrives in
 * -- absent from every list -- is the one state a guard cannot miss.
 * The gate map routes every test by area, and a test registered in
 * no area fails the map's own gate. Where a guard reads the tree,
 * it reads through a mirror and refuses wrong paths with a stated
 * reason; a meta-gate feeds every guard a set of decoy trees --
 * hollowed, reshaped, emptied, absent -- and requires sixty-nine
 * guards to succeed on the real tree and refuse three hundred odd
 * wrong ones, each refusal saying why. Guards are themselves
 * sabotage-tested: the way to trust a gate is to break the thing it
 * holds and watch it go red, because a gate that has never been seen
 * red is indistinguishable from one that cannot go red.
 *
 * The discipline the numbers come from: measurements land in commit
 * messages with their machines and inputs; bounds a gate holds are
 * stated in counts and ratios rather than seconds, so they survive
 * a loaded machine; and a claim this document makes that the suite
 * can check, the suite checks.
 *
 * @section design_perf Performance characteristics
 *
 * The interpreter's cost model, stated as properties of the current
 * design. The governing rule everywhere is that a cost grows with
 * the thing being done, never with the size of something standing
 * nearby: not with stack depth, not with everything ever freed, not
 * with the page's height when only a band of it is wanted.
 *
 * Costs that are constant. An allocation's free-list search (a
 * bounded scan of bounded buckets); the guard on an operator call's
 * operand count (read off the top segment); reaching the top of any
 * stack (the root names the top segment); asking whether a record
 * should spill (a running count, not a walk); the per-pixel halftone
 * question (one compiled site, cell geometry derived per screen
 * change); the names the marking paths use (resolved at install
 * time -- a per-span or per-string name walk is a cost no algorithm
 * underneath it can win back).
 *
 * Costs that are linear in their own work. @c roll, @c copy and
 * @c counttomark in the depth they touch; path building in the
 * path's length (the layout check resumes where it last left off);
 * a restore in what it must actually undo (copy-on-write records
 * plus a file-closing sweep driven by a census, so a restore that
 * closes nothing walks nothing); a scanline fill in the total edge
 * height rather than edges times page height; a band replay in the
 * entries whose row range meets the band.
 *
 * Where the hot loop's cycles go. Procedure elements step through
 * the flattened walker without re-entering the interpreter; the
 * twenty commonest operators run inline there when their operands
 * sit in the top segment; names resolve through the
 * generation-checked cache; a lone zero-operand C signature -- the
 * shape of nearly half the calls a rendering job makes -- dispatches
 * directly; type dispatch is a predictable switch. @c bind descends
 * through packed procedures, so generated programs run fully bound.
 * Release builds carry link-time optimisation, which is worth more
 * than any single algorithmic choice in the hot loop.
 *
 * The stated trades. Banding costs about twice direct painting on a
 * page that is almost entirely marks, and a gate holds the ratio at
 * 23/10 -- a ratio, not a time, because a time would be a statement
 * about the machine the suite runs on. Spilling trades memory for a
 * pass over the file per band. Page return trades residency now for
 * refaults later, and runs only when asked. Compaction moves every
 * live byte, and runs only when asked. The image boot costs a file
 * read; a refused image costs the boot it would have saved and
 * nothing else. Collection pacing is a measured threshold: wall
 * time is flat from about a megabyte of allocation between
 * collections upward, so the default sits at four megabytes and the
 * program or the environment may move it.
 *
 * Bounds a gate holds are stated in counts and ratios -- name
 * lookups per span, replay ratio, bytes per row -- because a count
 * survives a loaded machine and a second is a fact about one host.
 *
 * @section design_practice Working in the sources
 *
 * The conventions a change has to keep, and the knobs it can turn.
 *
 * <b>Do not hold a pointer while allocating.</b> This is the coding rule the
 * indexed design buys its freedom with, and it applies to device operator
 * functions as much as to the core. Real @c char* pointers into the arena are
 * available and the device sources use them; what a holder may not then do is
 * create a composite -- no new array, string or dictionary, no growing a
 * dictionary past its maxlength, no pushing a stack past its current segment,
 * no new name (a name allocates both characters and a stack slot) -- because
 * any of those may grow the memory file and move it. Lines commented
 * @c //recalc show how to refresh a pointer where one genuinely must be held
 * across such a call.
 *
 * Allocating is not the only thing that invalidates a pointer. Compaction
 * moves the bytes an entity holds, so every pointer derived from an entity's
 * recorded address is stale afterwards however it was obtained -- which is
 * why compaction happens in the outermost loop and nowhere else
 * (@ref design_return).
 *
 * <b>Raw and banked come in pairs.</b> The array, string and dictionary
 * functions each have two forms: a raw one that is told which memory file to
 * work in, and a banked one that reads the bank flag out of the object's own
 * tag and works it out. The raw one carries a @c _memory suffix:
 *
 * @verbatim
 * xpost_array_get_memory(mem, a, i)   raw -- caller names the bank
 * xpost_array_get(ctx, a, i)          banked -- read off the tag
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * and the same pairing holds for the puts, the constructors, and the string
 * and dictionary equivalents.
 *
 * <b>Say whether a new composite is executable.</b> The constructors return
 * objects already marked executable, which is rarely what a caller means.
 * State it rather than inheriting it:
 *
 * @verbatim
 * Xpost_Object arr = xpost_object_cvx(xpost_array_cons(ctx, 12));   executable
 * Xpost_Object arr = xpost_object_cvlit(xpost_array_cons(ctx, 12)); literal
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * <b>Terminology.</b> A datum in the arena is an @e entity when what is meant
 * is the bytes themselves, and an @e entry when what is meant is the table row
 * describing them -- its address, its size, its marks. An @e ent is the index
 * of that row. The special entities are spelt @c XPOST_MEMORY_TABLE_SPECIAL_FREE
 * and so on in @c xpost_memory.h, with @c XPOST_MEMORY_COLLECT_START_LOCAL and
 * @c ..._GLOBAL naming the first row past them in each bank.
 *
 * @subsection design_tunables Tunable constants
 *
 * @verbatim
 * XPOST_STACK_SEGMENT_SIZE            xpost_stack.h    objects per segment (1000)
 * XPOST_FREE_NBUCKETS                 xpost_free.h     size-class buckets (16)
 * XPOST_FREE_SCAN_LIMIT               xpost_free.h     entries scanned per bucket (8)
 * XPOST_GARBAGE_COLLECTION_THRESHOLD  xpost_free.h     bytes between collections
 *                                                      (4,000,000; XPOST_GC_THRESHOLD
 *                                                      overrides)
 * XPOST_RECORD_NEST                   xpost_record.h   form placement depth (12)
 * XPOST_REPLAY_BATCH                  xpost_dev_record.c  marks per replay turn (512)
 * RECORD_WEIGH_EVERY                  xpost_record.c   marks between spill weighings (64)
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * @subsection design_matrix Matrices
 *
 * The PostScript matrix @c [a @c b @c c @c d @c e @c f] is held internally as
 * the transpose of the diagrams in the PLRM. The results are identical; what
 * the transpose buys is right-multiplication, which is the natural direction
 * in C, where PostScript's left-multiplying model is the natural one for its
 * own reverse-Polish syntax.
 *
 * @subsection design_logging Logging
 *
 * The interpreter's own messages are governed by @c XPOST_LOG_LEVEL, an
 * integer: 0 errors, 1 warnings, 2 information, 3 debug. The debug level is
 * voluminous enough that redirecting it to a file is usually the only way to
 * read it.
 *
 * @subsection design_sourcetree Running out of the source tree
 *
 * The five-step search for the data directory (@ref design_langsplit) is what
 * makes an uninstalled build self-sufficient. Two of the five do that work:
 * the directory derived from the shared library's own location -- an installed
 * library looks beside itself for @c ../share/xpost, and failing that tries
 * @c ../../../data, which is the source tree's data directory from either
 * @c build/src/lib or @c src/lib/.libs -- and, for a build directory anywhere
 * else, the source tree's data directory recorded absolutely at configure
 * time. So an interpreter built and not installed finds the tree's PostScript
 * files from any working directory, and an edit to one takes effect without
 * installing anything. An alias is enough to keep one to hand:
 *
 * @verbatim
 * alias xpost="$PWD/build/src/bin/xpost"
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * The same fallback is a trap for anything that reads the tree
 * programmatically. A guard handed the wrong source root does not fail: it
 * finds a working tree by one of those routes and reports a perfectly true
 * result about a tree the caller did not mean. tests/guard-paths.sh exists for
 * that reason, and doc/CONTRIBUTING.md says how a guard is expected to use it.
 *
 * @section design_distinctives Distinctives
 *
 * The properties that most define this implementation, stated
 * without comparison:
 *
 * A page's memory is bounded by policy -- record, band, budget,
 * spill -- under a byte-identity guarantee between the banded and
 * whole-page routes, held to one row. Virtual memory is indexed
 * rather than addressed, and everything follows from that: it
 * compacts, it returns storage to the operating system, and it
 * boots from an image of itself. The build has two object widths
 * with the whole suite run at both; an optional face library whose
 * absence is loud and contractual; a graphics system implemented in
 * the language it serves over a sealed core; full enforcement of
 * the language's access attributes; and cross-boundary references
 * that are checked numbers, never trusted addresses. And the
 * documentation is load-bearing: the rosters, registers and stated
 * bounds in this page and its companions are read by the test
 * suite, so a change that falsifies them fails a gate rather than
 * aging the prose.
 */
